# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for the builds that link the release
# libnl_capi.a (tests/vm/run.sh, real-tsan).
#
# The Rust library is not instrumented, so TSan sees none of its
# synchronization: not its atomics, and not the futex calls that park its
# threads on Linux. It still sees the malloc, free, memcpy and memcmp calls
# the library makes, and memory passed between the compiler thread and the
# loader over std::sync::mpsc then looks unsynchronized.
#
# TSan only records frames of instrumented code, so a Rust function shows up
# in a report only as the direct caller of one of those intercepted calls.
# These patterns match Rust function names and no C function name, so they
# suppress exactly the reports where one side is the Rust library. Races
# between C accesses are still reported.
#
# Demangled Rust names contain "::" (clang's TSan runtime demangles them).
race:::
# Mangled ones start with _R (v0 mangling) or _ZN (legacy mangling); GCC's
# TSan runtime prints v0 names mangled.
race:^_R
race:^_ZN
